## 2. Task 1 — Multi-language support (English, Hindi, Bengali, Marathi, Telugu, Tamil)

**Goal:** every citizen-facing screen (not the admin console, which can stay English-only
for v1) is fully translatable and defaults to the browser/OS language when supported,
otherwise English, with a persistent user-chosen override.

1. Add `react-i18next` + `i18next` + `i18next-browser-languagedetector` to
   `package.json` dependencies (do not add heavier i18n frameworks; this stack is the
   lightest fit for TanStack Start + Vite).
2. Create `src/i18n/` with:
   - `src/i18n/index.ts` — i18next init, `supportedLngs: ['en','hi','bn','mr','te','ta']`,
     `fallbackLng: 'en'`, language detector reading `localStorage` key
     `sahayak_lang` first, then `navigator.language`.
   - `src/i18n/locales/en/common.json`, `hi/common.json`, `bn/common.json`,
     `mr/common.json`, `te/common.json`, `ta/common.json` — one namespace `common` to
     start, containing every hardcoded string currently in:
     `src/routes/dashboard.tsx`, `assistant.tsx`, `documents.tsx`, `applications.index.tsx`,
     `applications.$id.tsx`, `schemes.tsx`, `notifications.tsx`, `profile.tsx`, `login.tsx`,
     `signup.tsx`, `index.tsx`, and `src/components/sahayak/*.tsx`.
   - Use proper native scripts for translations (Devanagari for Hindi/Marathi, Bengali
     script, Telugu script, Tamil script) — do not transliterate into Latin characters.
     Numbers/currency stay in the `₹` / Indian numbering convention for all locales.
3. Replace every hardcoded JSX string in the files above with `t('key')` via
   `useTranslation()`. Do **not** touch copy inside `backend/` (server-side LLM prompts
   stay in English — the LLM system prompts are not user-facing).
4. Add a `LanguageSwitcher` component in `src/components/sahayak/components.tsx`,
   rendered in `AppShell`'s top bar (citizen layout) and in `login.tsx`/`signup.tsx` before
   authentication, so language can be picked before login. Persist the choice to
   `localStorage.sahayak_lang` and, if authenticated, also patch it onto
   `profiles.preferred_language` (see migration below) so it round-trips across devices.
5. **New additive migration** `supabase/migrations/<timestamp>_add_preferred_language.sql`:
   ```sql
   alter table public.profiles
     add column if not exists preferred_language text not null default 'en'
     check (preferred_language in ('en','hi','bn','mr','te','ta'));
   ```
6. Update `src/routes/__root.tsx` (or wherever `<html lang="en">` is set) to set
   `lang` dynamically from the active i18next language, and set `dir="ltr"` explicitly
   (all six languages are LTR, so no RTL work is needed, but keep the attribute explicit
   for future languages).
7. Any AI-generated **citizen-facing** text that currently comes from the backend (agent
   event `action` strings written by `write_agent_event(...)` in `agent_graph.py`, and
   LLM `explanation`/`message` fields) should stay in English at the data layer, but the
   frontend must wrap the *fixed-shape* system messages (e.g. "Application draft #{id}
   created and ready for citizen approval.") in a translation lookup table keyed by a
   stable `event_code` rather than trying to machine-translate free-form LLM text live.
   Concretely: extend `backend/agent_graph.py`'s `write_agent_event` calls to also pass a
   short stable `event_code` (e.g. `"APPLICATION_DRAFT_CREATED"`) in the `details` jsonb
   payload alongside the existing English `action` string (additive field, do not remove
   `action`). The frontend's `ActivityList`/journey step renderer looks up
   `details.event_code` in the i18n bundle first and falls back to the raw `action` string
   if no `event_code` is present (covers old rows and truly dynamic LLM prose).

**Acceptance criteria:** switching the language selector immediately re-renders the
dashboard, assistant, documents, applications, schemes, notifications, and profile pages
in the chosen language with no page reload; refreshing the page keeps the chosen language;
an unauthenticated visitor can pick a language on `/login` before signing in.

