## 9. Task 8 — Agent-event write security (RLS fix)

This is a real, exploitable finding, not stylistic: the current policy

```sql
create policy "agent_events_insert_all" on public.agent_events
  for insert with check (
    exists (
      select 1 from public.agent_runs r
      where r.id = run_id and (r.citizen_id = auth.uid() or public.is_admin())
    )
  );
```

lets a citizen's own browser session (their Supabase anon-key JWT) insert directly into
`agent_events` for any `run_id` they own, forging fake AI-agent messages that would then
appear as legitimate live events in their own timeline and — worse — feed into the audit
narrative an admin later reviews.

1. **New additive migration** `supabase/migrations/<timestamp>_lock_agent_events_writes.sql`:
   ```sql
   drop policy if exists "agent_events_insert_all" on public.agent_events;

   -- Only the backend service role may write agent_events. Citizens/admins may only read
   -- (existing "agent_events_via_run" select policy stays unchanged).
   create policy "agent_events_insert_service_role_only" on public.agent_events
     for insert with check (auth.jwt()->>'role' = 'service_role');
   ```
2. Audit every frontend call site that currently inserts into `agent_events` directly
   from the browser client (`src/lib/services.ts`'s `understandCitizenNeed()` does exactly
   this today via `supabase.from("agent_events").insert(...)` using the citizen's own
   session) and **remove those direct inserts**. Citizen-initiated intent events must
   instead be recorded by the backend (`orchestrate-agent-run` Edge Function already
   creates the `agent_runs` row and forwards to the real backend graph, which already
   calls `write_agent_event` with the service role key — the frontend's local intent
   classification in `services.ts` becomes purely a client-side UX hint, no longer a
   database write). Keep `understandCitizenNeed()`'s local category-detection heuristic for
   optimistic UI only; delete its Supabase insert block.
3. Apply the same audit to `audit_logs` (already correctly locked to admin/service_role —
   verify no frontend code path attempts to insert there; if any does, remove it) and to
   `agent_runs` (citizens are allowed to `insert`/read their own runs per existing policy —
   that's fine, since `status` transitions are still driven by the backend/service role
   on `update`; double check no frontend code ever calls `.update()` on `agent_runs`
   directly — grep confirms it currently does not, keep it that way).

**Acceptance criteria:** attempting `supabase.from('agent_events').insert(...)` from a
citizen-authenticated browser session (write a test for this, Task 10) fails with a
Postgres RLS error; the real orchestration flow (backend writing via service role)
continues to work exactly as before.

